// expect-ok
// Z3 corpus #14: axis-1 — deadline-typed (parametric) caps, Stage 1.
//
// HISTORY
//
// Through Wall 1's closure, this fixture was `expect-error`: the
// parser refused `cap type Approval(deadline_ms: i64) {}` with
// "expected `{` or `;` after capability type name". The wall existed
// to make the absence of parametric cap types honest in the corpus.
//
// Wall 2 Stage 1 (this fixture's flip) delivered the bounded first
// step described in the original WALL section's acceptance criteria:
//
//   1. Grammar accepts `cap type <Name>(<param>: i64) { ... }`.
//   2. Type-checker tracks the parameter as part of cap's type
//      identity: `Approval(2030_06_01) ≠ Approval(2025_06_01)`.
//   3. Cap construction (restrict / split / draw) propagates the
//      parameter; the output cap's deadline equals the source's.
//   4. Function signatures, spawn init parameters, state fields,
//      message handler parameters, let-binding annotations, and
//      `Slot<T>` instantiation all accept literal-deadline cap types.
//   5. Subtyping rule: `Approval(D_a) <: Approval(D_b)` iff
//      `D_a >= D_b` (covariance — longer deadline is more permissive).
//      Subtype-incompatible flows fire **T195**.
//
// WHAT THIS FIXTURE NOW PROVES
//
// A small parametric program type-checks cleanly: a parametric cap
// type is declared, a function takes a literal-deadline parametric
// argument, and a state field uses the same shape. The fixture also
// exercises the covariant subtyping rule by passing a longer-deadline
// cap (`Approval(2031)`) into a `Approval(2030)`-typed sink.
//
// STAGES 2 AND 3 (now shipped — the wall is closed)
//
//   * Stage 2 (`--build-deadline` + `restrict_deadline`): the CLI flag
//     `sigil check --build-deadline <N>` makes the compiler refuse any
//     parametric cap literal `Cap(D)` with `D < N` (T199). The new
//     `cap.restrict_deadline(D')` method narrows a parametric cap's
//     declared deadline at the call site, consuming the source and
//     producing a fresh `Cap(name, min(D_orig, D'))`. Extension or
//     non-parametric receivers fire T200.
//   * Stage 3 (close the narrowing escape hatch): `restrict_deadline`
//     arguments are method-call literals, not type positions — Stage
//     2's T199 didn't see them. Stage 3 mirrors the build-deadline
//     check at the restrict_deadline call site so a user can't narrow
//     past `BUILD_NOW` through the method-call door either.
//
// What the original WALL section's ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA contemplated
// for a future "Stage 4" — Z3 QF_LIA modelling of deadlines that
// would let fixture `11_time_bound_approval.sigil`'s runtime
// `time_now()` FFI guard become optional — is intentionally NOT
// shipped. The realized deadline-typed cap story stops at the type
// system: deadlines are unforgeable type identities, the build
// pipeline gates literal values, runtime narrowing is constrained
// by the same gate. Fixture 11's runtime check stays at runtime —
// it now serves only to catch executions that drift past
// `BUILD_NOW + lifetime`, never because the compiler missed a
// type-level violation.
//
// THREAT-MODEL STANCE (STAGE 1)
//
// Deadline integrity in Stage 1 rests on source-fingerprint trust
// (the verification certificate, schema v3, SHA-256). The runtime
// cap-record metadata is unchanged — there is no per-cap deadline
// field in the wasm representation. Use-after-deadline must still be
// gated at runtime by the policy author (see fixture #11). Stage 1's
// contribution is that the TYPE SYSTEM now refuses to let the policy
// author hand a cap to a sink whose declared deadline doesn't match
// the cap's declared deadline — that "right argument, right shape"
// guarantee is what makes the runtime check (when present) provably
// comparing against the correct value.
//
// MUTATION CONTRACT
//
// Not applicable. This is an `expect-ok` fixture (positive evidence
// of expressiveness). Removing any line would break compilation, not
// flip a verdict.
module sigil;

cap type Approval(deadline_ms: i64) {}

fn use_before_2030(c: Approval(2030)) -> i64 {
    return 1;
}

entry actor Main {
    state { authority: Approval(2031) }

    on Start() -> i64 {
        // Covariance: a cap valid until 2031 is acceptable wherever
        // a 2030-bounded cap is required. The type system accepts.
        return use_before_2030(authority);
    }
}
